    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d710">

                <pb facs="0773" n="763" sameAs="frus:frus1944v04/pg763"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00112 European War 1939/8–3144:
                    Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Switzerland</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Harrison</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Bern</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-08-31T22:00:00+01:00">August 31, 1944—10
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received September 1—9 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>5710. After receipt your 2958 August 26 I asked for appointment. Mr. Pilet
                    received me this morning and I made informal <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >démarche</hi> outlined your 2876. My British colleague with whom I had
                    concerted action had previously seen Mr. Pilet.</p>
                <p>Mr. Pilet told me that he had spoken to President Stampfli and that question of
                    exports to Axis would be taken up at meeting of Federal Council next
                        Tuesday.<anchor n="4" xml:id="d710-f1"/> He had also spoken to Dr. Hotz. Mr.
                    Pilet gave me to understand that he will urge on his colleagues impression [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">importance?</hi>] of taking prompt advantage of recent
                    developments to give notice to Germans under escape clause. I therefore suggest
                    that the outcome Tuesday be awaited.</p>
                <pb facs="0774" n="764" xml:id="pg764"/>
                <p>During our conversation Mr. Pilet urged that it would strengthen his hand and
                    also be convincing evidence of ineffectiveness of counter blockade if passage
                    via France by truck for Swiss products of small bulk such as watches and
                    precision instruments could now be permitted. My recommendation will depend upon
                    Tuesday’s decision.</p>
                <p>As regards transit traffic Mr. Pilet told me that details re proposed additional
                    controls would be given by Mr. Hotz to OA and CS.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Harrison</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
